    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 222147\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n247 PM PDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Cooler temperatures and lingering light showers and isolated \n  thunderstorms for portions of the southern Great Basin this \n  afternoon.\n\n* Above normal temperatures return midweek onward, with a return\n  of thunderstorm chances for Mohave County and the Lower \n  Colorado River Valley late week.\n\n* Potential pattern shift early next week could bring cooler\n  temperatures and precipitation chances to the area.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through Monday.\n\nThe shortwave responsible for yesterday's unsettled weather and \ntoday's cooler temperatures continues its eastward progression\ninto Utah this afternoon. Near the core of this system, \nincreased ascent and lingering wrap-around moisture is expected to \nyield isolated showers and perhaps a thunderstorm or two this \nafternoon, primarily across the southern Great Basin, including \nportions of central Nye County and northern Lincoln County. \nShowers and storms that develop will be capable of producing light\nto moderate rainfall, brief gusty winds, and lightning strikes, \nthough flooding and strong winds are not expected. There is a \nlower (10-20%) chance for additional showers this afternoon over \nthe Spring Mountains and Sheep Range in Clark County where \ntopography will aid in ascent in the wake of the departing system.\nOtherwise, expect temperatures to top out around 2 to 5 degrees \nbelow normal for most locations across the region this afternoon, \na fitting start to the astronomical fall season!\n\nMidweek onward, increasing thicknesses in the wake of the\ndeparting shortwave will allow for a gradual warming trend, with\nhigh temperatures warming to around 5 degrees above normal by \nFriday and Saturday into the upper 90s and lower 100s across \ndesert valleys (the normal high in Las Vegas this time of year is \n91 degrees). Fortunately, only Minor (and localized Moderate) \nHeatRisk will result. Additionally, while most of the region will \nstay dry, ensembles and deterministic guidance are in agreement \nthat a secondary shortwave will pinch off of the aforementioned \nwave, lingering off of the southern California/Baja Coast before \nejecting northeastward and becoming absorbed in the mean flow late\nweek into the weekend. This feature will be accompanied by an \nuptick in precipitable water values, mainly south and east of \nI-15, as well as a return of thunderstorm chances to southeastern \nportions of the area as early as Thursday afternoon. Weak forcing \nwill limit convective activity both spatially and temporally, \nhowever, isolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms look to \ndevelop over the higher terrain of Mohave County later this week, \nwith some uncertainty regarding whether convection will linger \nover Mohave County beyond Thursday. \n\nLate weekend into early next week, ensembles and cluster analyses  \nare indicating a potential longwave trough that will deepen along \nthe West Coast before slowly progressing inland. However, details\nremain highly uncertain at this time with discrepancy among\nguidance regarding the amplitude and timing of this feature. If \nthis pattern shift materializes, another round of cooler \ntemperatures can be expected, along with a return of precipitation\nchances for portions of the region. So, stay tuned.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 00Z Forecast Package...Winds\nthrough Wednesday evening will follow typical daily directional \npatterns with some variability, with speeds remaining under 8KT. \nVFR conditions prevail with increasing high clouds late in the \nforecast period.\n\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 00Z Forecast Package...Winds across the\nregion will remain around 5-10KT and follow typical daily \ndirectional patterns with some variability the next 24 hours. The \nonly exception is along the Lower Colorado River Valley in the \nvicinity of KIFP, where down-valley northerly winds in the morning\nwill briefly become elevated around 10-12KT with sporadic higher \ngusts possible. Isolated showers and thunderstorms are expected \nacross the higher terrain of the southern Great Basin through \nsunset this evening, with gusty winds and lightning in the \nvicinity of storms the main concerns, and no impacts expected at \narea terminals. VFR conditions otherwise prevail with no \noperationally significant cloud cover expected. \n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION/AVIATION...Phillipson\n\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
